<Base
  title="DAL-X — Runtime Authority Framework — Jochanni Labs"
  description="DAL-X is the emerging runtime authority framework for AI-influenced decision paths — defining trigger logic, authority routing, execution gating, decision recording, and audit evidence. Developed by Kevin Moore and Jochanni Labs."
>

  <!-- ── Page Header ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="dalx-header">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="dalx-header-inner">

        <div class="dalx-header-content">
          <p class="dalx-header-label">Runtime Authority Framework</p>
          <h1 class="dalx-header-mark">DAL-X</h1>
          <p class="dalx-header-sub">Kevin Moore &nbsp;·&nbsp; Jochanni Labs</p>
          <p class="dalx-header-body">
            The emerging runtime authority framework for AI-influenced decision paths.
            Developed to close the structural gap between AI output and authorized
            organizational action — before consequence occurs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="dalx-id-card">
          <p class="dalx-id-mark">DAL-X</p>
          <div class="dalx-id-lines">
            <p>Decision Authority Layer</p>
            <p>Runtime Execution Control</p>
            <p>Jochanni Labs</p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <main>

    <!-- ── Section 01 — What DAL-X Means ───────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="dalx-section">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="dalx-section-head">
          <p class="dalx-section-marker">01 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Definition</p>
          <h2 class="dalx-section-title">What DAL-X Means</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="dalx-two-col">
          <div class="dalx-body">
            <p>
              DAL-X is the Decision Authority Layer — the runtime governing structure
              that determines whether AI-influenced work is authorized to proceed before
              it produces organizational consequence. The X designates the execution
              point: the moment at which authority must be active, or the decision path
              is ungoverned.
            </p>
            <p>
              DAL-X is not a product category. It is not a compliance framework. It is
              not a monitoring dashboard or a model governance protocol. It is the
              definitional framework for a discipline that does not yet have a name in
              most enterprises: the runtime authority layer that governs AI-influenced
              decisions at the moment they would move toward consequence.
            </p>
            <p>
              Kevin Moore and Jochanni Labs are developing DAL-X as the foundational
              research initiative for this discipline — defining the vocabulary,
              architecture, and operational requirements of Decision Governance before
              regulatory pressure, organizational incident, or competitive disadvantage
              forces the issue.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="dalx-acronym-card">
            <div class="dalx-acronym-row">
              <span class="dalx-acronym-letter">D</span>
              <div class="dalx-acronym-def">
                <span class="dalx-acronym-word">Decision</span>
                <span class="dalx-acronym-note">The governed object — not the model</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-acronym-sep"></div>
            <div class="dalx-acronym-row">
              <span class="dalx-acronym-letter">A</span>
              <div class="dalx-acronym-def">
                <span class="dalx-acronym-word">Authority</span>
                <span class="dalx-acronym-note">Named, mapped, enforced before action</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-acronym-sep"></div>
            <div class="dalx-acronym-row">
              <span class="dalx-acronym-letter">L</span>
              <div class="dalx-acronym-def">
                <span class="dalx-acronym-word">Layer</span>
                <span class="dalx-acronym-note">Active infrastructure, not policy document</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-acronym-sep"></div>
            <div class="dalx-acronym-row dalx-acronym-row--accent">
              <span class="dalx-acronym-letter">X</span>
              <div class="dalx-acronym-def">
                <span class="dalx-acronym-word">Execution Point</span>
                <span class="dalx-acronym-note">The moment governance must be present</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 02 — Why DAL-X Exists ────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="dalx-section dalx-section--raised">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="dalx-section-head">
          <p class="dalx-section-marker">02 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Origin</p>
          <h2 class="dalx-section-title">Why DAL-X Exists</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="dalx-body dalx-body--wide">
          <p>
            AI systems are participating in consequential organizational decisions.
            They write contracts, classify risk, route customers, recommend exceptions,
            and approve actions — increasingly at machine speed, inside workflows that
            were designed for human-speed work. The authority structures governing those
            decisions have not kept pace.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every enterprise has formal authority requirements for consequential
            decisions: approval rights, escalation thresholds, supervisory review,
            evidence obligations. Those requirements exist in policy documents, in
            control frameworks, in audit protocols. What most enterprises do not have
            is the operational layer that enforces those requirements at the moment
            AI-influenced work would move toward consequence.
          </p>
          <p>
            That absent layer is what DAL-X names and what the DAL-X framework is
            being built to define. The problem is not that enterprises lack policies.
            The problem is that policies without runtime enforcement are intent, not
            governance. DAL-X closes the structural gap between governance as written
            and governance as active.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="dalx-gap-visual">
          <div class="dalx-gap-side">
            <p class="dalx-gap-label dalx-gap-label--dim">Without Runtime Authority</p>
            <div class="dalx-flow-simple">
              <div class="dalx-fs-node dalx-fs-node--neutral">AI Output</div>
              <div class="dalx-fs-gap">
                <span class="dalx-fs-gap-label">authority gap</span>
              </div>
              <div class="dalx-fs-node dalx-fs-node--action">Human Action</div>
              <div class="dalx-fs-arrow">→</div>
              <div class="dalx-fs-node dalx-fs-node--consequence">Consequence</div>
            </div>
            <p class="dalx-gap-note">
              AI output moves directly toward action. No mechanism exists to
              confirm the decision path was authorized before consequence occurred.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="dalx-gap-divider"></div>
          <div class="dalx-gap-side dalx-gap-side--accent">
            <p class="dalx-gap-label dalx-gap-label--orange">With DAL-X</p>
            <div class="dalx-flow-simple">
              <div class="dalx-fs-node dalx-fs-node--neutral">AI Output</div>
              <div class="dalx-fs-arrow dalx-fs-arrow--orange">→</div>
              <div class="dalx-fs-node dalx-fs-node--authority">Runtime Authority</div>
              <div class="dalx-fs-arrow dalx-fs-arrow--orange">→</div>
              <div class="dalx-fs-node dalx-fs-node--gate">Authorized Action</div>
            </div>
            <p class="dalx-gap-note">
              Authority is active at the execution point. The decision path cannot
              move forward without scope, conformance, and acknowledgment confirmed.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 03 — The Runtime Authority Problem ───────────────── -->
    <section class="dalx-section">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="dalx-section-head">
          <p class="dalx-section-marker">03 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Architecture</p>
          <h2 class="dalx-section-title">The Runtime Authority Problem</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="dalx-body dalx-body--wide">
          <p>
            The runtime authority problem is structural. AI-influenced work enters
            an organization's decision path at the point of output. From that point,
            the path moves toward action — through human review, automated routing,
            manager approval, or direct execution. At each step, the question of
            whether the AI participation was authorized, and whether the decision
            path had standing to proceed at that consequence level, goes unasked
            and unanswered.
          </p>
          <p>
            Runtime authority does not mean slowing every decision. It means that
            for each decision path where AI participates, the organization has
            defined — and is actively enforcing — the authority requirements at the
            moment the decision would take effect. Scope: was AI allowed here?
            Conformance: does the path meet the governing framework? Acknowledgment:
            has the right principal accepted responsibility?
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Authority flow diagram -->
        <div class="dalx-flow-wrap">
          <div class="dalx-flow">
            <div class="dalx-flow-node dalx-flow-node--input">
              <p class="dalx-flow-node-label">AI Output</p>
              <p class="dalx-flow-node-sub">Recommendation<br>Classification<br>Action proposal</p>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-flow-connector">
              <div class="dalx-flow-line"></div>
              <div class="dalx-flow-arrowhead"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-flow-node dalx-flow-node--trigger">
