            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="dalx-flow-caption">
          The DAL-X authority chain — AI output through runtime governance to authorized action.
          Each node is a required layer; removing any one breaks governance continuity.
        </p>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 04 — Trigger Logic ───────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="dalx-section dalx-section--raised">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="dalx-section-head">
          <p class="dalx-section-marker">04 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Mechanism</p>
          <h2 class="dalx-section-title">Trigger Logic</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="dalx-two-col dalx-two-col--flip">

          <div class="dalx-trigger-table">
            <p class="dalx-diagram-label">Trigger Condition Evaluation</p>
            <div class="dalx-trigger-row dalx-trigger-row--active">
              <span class="dalx-trigger-indicator"></span>
              <span class="dalx-trigger-condition">AI participation detected in decision path</span>
              <span class="dalx-trigger-fires">FIRES</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-trigger-row dalx-trigger-row--active">
              <span class="dalx-trigger-indicator"></span>
              <span class="dalx-trigger-condition">Consequence level at or above defined threshold</span>
              <span class="dalx-trigger-fires">FIRES</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-trigger-row dalx-trigger-row--active">
              <span class="dalx-trigger-indicator"></span>
              <span class="dalx-trigger-condition">Risk classification requires supervisory review</span>
              <span class="dalx-trigger-fires">FIRES</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-trigger-row dalx-trigger-row--active">
              <span class="dalx-trigger-indicator"></span>
              <span class="dalx-trigger-condition">Jurisdictional authority requirement present</span>
              <span class="dalx-trigger-fires">FIRES</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-trigger-row dalx-trigger-row--inactive">
              <span class="dalx-trigger-indicator dalx-trigger-indicator--off"></span>
              <span class="dalx-trigger-condition">No AI participation · Below consequence threshold</span>
              <span class="dalx-trigger-pass">PASS</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-trigger-result">
              <span class="dalx-trigger-result-label">Governance Active — Authority Routing Engaged</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="dalx-body">
            <p>
              Trigger logic is the mechanism that converts AI participation into a
              governance event. Without trigger logic, AI output enters the decision
              path silently — indistinguishable from non-AI work, carrying no
              governance marker that would activate authority requirements.
            </p>
            <p>
              A trigger fires when a defined condition is met: the consequence level
              of the decision exceeds a threshold, the AI participation flag is
              present, the risk classification requires escalation, or the decision
              path enters a jurisdictional domain with specific authority requirements.
              When the trigger fires, governance becomes operational — not as a
              checklist to be completed after the fact, but as an active constraint
              on what the decision path is permitted to do next.
            </p>
            <p>
              Trigger logic is the boundary between AI governance as policy and
              AI governance as enforcement.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 05 — Authority Routing ───────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="dalx-section">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="dalx-section-head">
          <p class="dalx-section-marker">05 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Mechanism</p>
          <h2 class="dalx-section-title">Authority Routing</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="dalx-two-col">

          <div class="dalx-body">
            <p>
              Authority routing maps the triggered decision path to the correct
              principal — the individual or body with the authority level required
              to review and accept responsibility for AI-influenced work at the
              specific consequence level the decision carries.
            </p>
            <p>
              The routing is not generic. It maps the consequence level and risk
              profile of the specific AI-influenced decision path to the specific
              principal whose authority threshold matches. A decision that requires
              a senior compliance officer cannot be routed to a department manager.
              A decision above a regulatory threshold cannot be approved by a
              principal who lacks the standing to accept that authority level.
            </p>
            <p>
              Authority routing is what makes human oversight an actual control
              rather than a procedural step. The right principal, with the right
              authority, for the right decision — this is the operational requirement
              that most human-in-the-loop implementations do not satisfy.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="dalx-routing-diagram">
            <p class="dalx-diagram-label">Principal Authority Map</p>
            <div class="dalx-route-tier dalx-route-tier--3">
              <div>
                <span class="dalx-route-tier-label">Executive Authority</span>
              </div>
              <span class="dalx-route-tier-threshold">High consequence · Regulatory exposure</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-route-line"></div>
            <div class="dalx-route-tier dalx-route-tier--2">
              <div>
                <span class="dalx-route-tier-label">Supervisory Authority</span>
              </div>
              <span class="dalx-route-tier-threshold">Elevated consequence · Policy exception</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-route-line"></div>
            <div class="dalx-route-tier dalx-route-tier--1">
              <div>
                <span class="dalx-route-tier-label">Operational Authority</span>
              </div>
              <span class="dalx-route-tier-threshold">Standard consequence · Defined scope</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-route-line"></div>
            <div class="dalx-route-tier dalx-route-tier--0">
              <div>
                <span class="dalx-route-tier-label">Automated Path</span>
              </div>
              <span class="dalx-route-tier-threshold">Below threshold · Pre-authorized scope</span>
            </div>
            <p class="dalx-routing-note">
              Consequence level determines routing tier.
              Misrouting is a governance failure — not a process error.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 06 — Execution Gating ────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="dalx-section dalx-section--raised">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="dalx-section-head">
          <p class="dalx-section-marker">06 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Mechanism</p>
          <h2 class="dalx-section-title">Execution Gating</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="dalx-two-col dalx-two-col--flip">

          <div class="dalx-gate-diagram">
            <p class="dalx-diagram-label">Execution Gate — Three Concurrent Requirements</p>
            <div class="dalx-gate-requirement dalx-gate-req--pass">
              <div class="dalx-gate-req-status">
                <span class="dalx-gate-req-indicator dalx-gate-req-indicator--pass"></span>
                <span class="dalx-gate-req-name">Scope</span>
              </div>
              <p class="dalx-gate-req-desc">
                AI participation is within authorized jurisdictional boundaries.
                The system had standing to participate at this consequence level.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-gate-requirement dalx-gate-req--pass">
              <div class="dalx-gate-req-status">
                <span class="dalx-gate-req-indicator dalx-gate-req-indicator--pass"></span>
                <span class="dalx-gate-req-name">Conformance</span>
              </div>
              <p class="dalx-gate-req-desc">
                Decision path satisfies all active governance framework requirements —
                policies, thresholds, and evidence obligations currently in effect.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-gate-requirement dalx-gate-req--pass">
              <div class="dalx-gate-req-status">
                <span class="dalx-gate-req-indicator dalx-gate-req-indicator--pass"></span>
                <span class="dalx-gate-req-name">Acknowledgment</span>
              </div>
              <p class="dalx-gate-req-desc">
                An accountable principal with correct authority has formally accepted
                responsibility before the decision path moves toward execution.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-gate-result dalx-gate-result--open">
              <span class="dalx-gate-result-label">Gate Open — Execution Authorized</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="dalx-body">
            <p>
              The execution gate is the point at which all three governance
              requirements must be satisfied simultaneously before the decision
              path is permitted to move toward consequence. Scope is confirmed,
              conformance is validated, and acknowledgment is recorded from the
              correct principal.
            </p>
            <p>
              The gate is not a sequential checklist. It is a concurrent
              requirement: all three must be active at the moment of execution.
              A decision path that satisfies scope and conformance but lacks
              principal acknowledgment has not passed the gate. A decision path
              where the acknowledging principal lacks the authority required for
              that consequence level has not passed the gate.
            </p>
            <p>
              Execution gating is what converts intent-based governance into
              enforced governance. The gate does not close when policy exists.
              It closes only when the specific requirements for this decision
              path, at this consequence level, have been satisfied.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 07 — Decision Recording ──────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="dalx-section">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="dalx-section-head">
          <p class="dalx-section-marker">07 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Mechanism</p>
          <h2 class="dalx-section-title">Decision Recording</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="dalx-two-col">

          <div class="dalx-body">
            <p>
              Decision recording is the capture of the authority record before
              execution moves forward. The record is not a post-action log. It is
              a pre-execution artifact that establishes what authority was active,
              which principal accepted responsibility, what governance requirements
              were satisfied, and when each of those conditions was confirmed —
              all before the decision path produced consequence.
            </p>
            <p>
              The distinction between pre-execution recording and post-execution
              audit is governance-critical. A record created after consequence
              can explain what happened. It cannot prove the decision path was
              authorized when control was still possible. Decision recording
              makes that proof available because the record precedes the action.
            </p>
            <p>
              The record is the evidence that governance was active, not
              retrospective. It is what an organization produces when a decision
              is challenged — internally, by a customer, by a regulator, or
              under legal scrutiny.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="dalx-record-card">
            <p class="dalx-diagram-label">Pre-Execution Decision Record</p>
            <div class="dalx-record-field">
              <span class="dalx-record-key">Decision Path ID</span>
              <span class="dalx-record-val dalx-record-val--mono">DG-2026-04871</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-record-field">
              <span class="dalx-record-key">AI Participation</span>
              <span class="dalx-record-val">Flagged · Classification model</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-record-field">
              <span class="dalx-record-key">Consequence Level</span>
              <span class="dalx-record-val">Tier 2 — Supervisory review required</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-record-field">
              <span class="dalx-record-key">Scope Status</span>
              <span class="dalx-record-val dalx-record-val--pass">Confirmed within boundaries</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-record-field">
              <span class="dalx-record-key">Conformance Status</span>
              <span class="dalx-record-val dalx-record-val--pass">Framework requirements satisfied</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-record-field">
              <span class="dalx-record-key">Acknowledging Principal</span>
              <span class="dalx-record-val">Supervisory authority — confirmed</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-record-field">
              <span class="dalx-record-key">Record Captured</span>
              <span class="dalx-record-val dalx-record-val--mono">Before execution</span>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-record-field dalx-record-field--final">
              <span class="dalx-record-key">Authorization Status</span>
              <span class="dalx-record-val dalx-record-val--authorized">AUTHORIZED</span>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Section 08 — Audit Evidence ──────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="dalx-section dalx-section--raised">
      <div class="container">

        <div class="dalx-section-head">
          <p class="dalx-section-marker">08 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Mechanism</p>
          <h2 class="dalx-section-title">Audit Evidence</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="dalx-body dalx-body--wide">
          <p>
            Audit evidence in the DAL-X framework is the durable record that
            demonstrates governance was active before consequence occurred. It answers
            the questions any challenge will produce: Was AI used in this decision?
            Was its use authorized? Who reviewed it? Under what authority? What was
            the consequence level? Was the governing framework in effect? Was the
            evidence captured before or after the decision moved forward?
          </p>
          <p>
            The distinction between instrumentation and evidence is structural.
            Instrumentation records what happened. Evidence proves what was authorized.
            An organization that has built monitoring infrastructure without a
            pre-execution authority record has instrumented its governance gap — it
            knows more about what went wrong than it can prove about what was permitted.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="dalx-evidence-wrap">
          <div class="dalx-evidence-chain">
            <div class="dalx-evidence-node">
              <p class="dalx-evidence-node-num">01</p>
              <p class="dalx-evidence-node-label">AI Participation Record</p>
              <p class="dalx-evidence-node-desc">Which system participated, at which step, with what output</p>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-evidence-connector">
              <div class="dalx-evidence-line"></div>
              <div class="dalx-evidence-arrowhead"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-evidence-node">
              <p class="dalx-evidence-node-num">02</p>
              <p class="dalx-evidence-node-label">Trigger Event Log</p>
              <p class="dalx-evidence-node-desc">What condition fired, at what threshold, with what timestamp</p>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-evidence-connector">
              <div class="dalx-evidence-line"></div>
              <div class="dalx-evidence-arrowhead"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-evidence-node">
              <p class="dalx-evidence-node-num">03</p>
              <p class="dalx-evidence-node-label">Principal Authority Record</p>
              <p class="dalx-evidence-node-desc">Who reviewed, under what authority level, with what mandate</p>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-evidence-connector">
              <div class="dalx-evidence-line"></div>
              <div class="dalx-evidence-arrowhead"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-evidence-node">
              <p class="dalx-evidence-node-num">04</p>
              <p class="dalx-evidence-node-label">Gate Clearance Record</p>
              <p class="dalx-evidence-node-desc">Scope, conformance, and acknowledgment — all confirmed before execution</p>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-evidence-connector">
              <div class="dalx-evidence-line"></div>
              <div class="dalx-evidence-arrowhead"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="dalx-evidence-node dalx-evidence-node--terminal">
              <p class="dalx-evidence-node-num">05</p>
